  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $\u2200\\,y\\in Y,$ $\u2203\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose ${2}^{1/4}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)={2}^{1/4}.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}={2}^{1/4}$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- II is a surjection. Given $y\\in R,$ choose $x=-\\sqrt[3]{y}\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h(-\\sqrt[3]{y})=-{(-\\sqrt[3]{y})}^{3}=y.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=-1.$ In fact ${x}^{2}+x>0$ for all $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
